{{short description|Overview article}}
'''Epigenetic effects of smoking''' concerns how [[epigenetics]] (heritable characteristics that do not involve changes in DNA sequence) contributes to the deleterious effects of [[smoking]]. Cigarette smoking has been found to affect global epigenetic regulation of transcription across tissue types. Studies have shown differences in epigenetic markers such as [[DNA methylation]], [[histone modification]]s and [[miRNA]] expression between smokers and non-smokers. Similar differences exist in children whose mothers smoked during pregnancy. These epigenetic effects are thought to be linked to many of negative health effects associated with smoking.<ref>{{cite journal
